Let me explain both approaches calmly, because juniors often pick one at random and then fight the consequences.

First, the shared rules. Any composite-key class, whether embeddable or id-class, MUST be `Serializable`, MUST have a public no-arg constructor, and MUST implement `equals` and `hashCode` based on all key fields. Hibernate uses `equals`/`hashCode` to locate entities in the persistence context (the identity map), so a broken `equals` silently breaks caching and merging.

The `@EmbeddedId` approach uses one key class annotated `@Embeddable` and one field on the entity annotated `@EmbeddedId`:

```java
@Embeddable
public class StatementId implements java.io.Serializable {
    private String accountId;
    private java.time.YearMonth period;
    // no-arg ctor, all-args ctor, equals, hashCode, getters
}

@Entity
public class MonthlyStatement {
    @EmbeddedId
    private StatementId id;   // the whole key as one object
    private java.math.BigDecimal closingBalance;
    // access parts via id.getAccountId(), id.getPeriod()
}
```

The advantage is that the key is a first-class object, which is clean when you pass keys around or when the key has behaviour. The slight cost is that JPQL must navigate through the key: `WHERE s.id.accountId = :acc`.

The `@IdClass` approach keeps the key columns as ordinary fields on the entity, each annotated `@Id`, and declares a separate mirror class in `@IdClass`:

```java
public class StatementKey implements java.io.Serializable {
    private String accountId;        // names + types mirror the @Id fields
    private java.time.YearMonth period;
    // no-arg ctor, equals, hashCode
}

@Entity
@IdClass(StatementKey.class)
public class MonthlyStatement {
    @Id private String accountId;    // separate @Id fields on the entity itself
    @Id private java.time.YearMonth period;
    private java.math.BigDecimal closingBalance;
}
```

The advantage is that fields sit directly on the entity, so JPQL reads naturally: `WHERE s.accountId = :acc AND s.period = :p`, and simple getters return the parts without hopping through a key object. The cost is duplication — the id fields exist on both the entity and the mirror key class, and they must stay in sync.

For either approach, you look an entity up by constructing the key object and passing it to `findById`:

```java
// EmbeddedId:
repo.findById(new StatementId("SAV2001", YearMonth.of(2026, 10)));
// IdClass:
repo.findById(new StatementKey("SAV2001", YearMonth.of(2026, 10)));
```

A few senior nuances. When a composite key also acts as a foreign key into a parent (a derived identity), `@MapsId` lets a `@ManyToOne` share a key column, which is common in join-table entities. For converting a value type like `YearMonth` to a database column, I register an `AttributeConverter`. And in a banking system I strongly prefer that composite keys be built from stable, immutable business values — an account id and a period never change, which is exactly what you want in a primary key. My default recommendation is `@EmbeddedId` because grouping the key into one serializable object reduces mistakes and models the identity as a real concept, and I reach for `@IdClass` only when I want the id fields to appear directly on the entity for simpler queries or to match a legacy schema.

